            "review_planning_B",
            "broader_review_uses",
            "2",
            "purpose"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::e9adf4f31d125dfc1bbdfa7d2028883ba70ceed68aa6e1c33667379122810282",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::032bf1216bdcd21e30041ecf": {
      "atom_id": "atom::032bf1216bdcd21e30041ecf",
      "source_handle": "P0438",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "Does not provide specific statistical effect sizes or hazard ratios for ICI response based on microbiome status. The association is described qualitatively. Specific bacterial strains linked to *positive* ICI response are mentioned generally (e.g., Bifidobacterium, Lactobacillus for radiotherapy, but less specific for ICIs compared to other reviews), though it notes 'specific communities' are beneficial.",
        "contribution": "Identifies that specific gut microbial communities are associated with favorable responses to ICIs, while dysbiosis correlates with reduced efficacy. Mentions that microbiota influences systemic inflammation and immune cell function, which determines ICI success.",
        "facet_id": "F1"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::592aefafd208990b34ed0f86ee32fd5bca51feaa3368169e179a15c1fe4dd9eb",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::346986a969f4910cd68c8399cc17ef413e8d76599d3cea59a0913d8a64bfd929",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::8f086eefc72f26dd0bddbca1": {
      "atom_id": "atom::8f086eefc72f26dd0bddbca1",
      "source_handle": "P0438",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions",
        "possible_uses"
      ],
      "value": "Can be used to support the claim that gut microbiome status is a determinant of ICI efficacy in solid tumors. Provides the general context that microbiome modulation is a strategy to improve ICI outcomes.",
      "role": "planning",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::592aefafd208990b34ed0f86ee32fd5bca51feaa3368169e179a15c1fe4dd9eb",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0",
            "possible_uses"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::0520e8e0919b8dfe49e849560087258393e41ea69f2975f48a7f741130e0325e",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::24038f680de80afec539f625": {
      "atom_id": "atom::24038f680de80afec539f625",
      "source_handle": "P0438",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "Many mechanistic details are derived from preclinical models (mouse models, organoids) or specific bacterial infections (H. pylori, B. fragilis) rather than direct ICI intervention studies in humans. The link between these specific inflammatory mechanisms and ICI resistance is implied but not always explicitly tested in the same cohort.",
        "contribution": "Details mechanisms by which microbiota modulates immunity: 1) Production of microbial byproducts (SCFAs, bile acids) regulating immune cells. 2) Training of immune cells and fostering diverse T-cells. 3) Specific examples like Barnesiella intestinihominis enhancing T-cell infiltration for chemotherapy. 4) Fusobacterium nucleatum inhibiting NK cell responses and recruiting MDSCs for immune evasion. 5) H. pylori and B. fragilis mechanisms involving cytokine release (IL-17, TNF-a) and signaling pathways (NF-kB, STAT3) that create an immunosuppressive or pro-inflammatory environment.",
        "facet_id": "F2"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::592aefafd208990b34ed0f86ee32fd5bca51feaa3368169e179a15c1fe4dd9eb",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::346986a969f4910cd68c8399cc17ef413e8d76599d3cea59a0913d8a64bfd929",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::5248725e486cc2731f400f82": {
      "atom_id": "atom::5248725e486cc2731f400f82",
      "source_handle": "P0438",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions",
        "possible_uses"
      ],
      "value": "Provides specific molecular and cellular mechanisms (MDSC recruitment, NK inhibition, T-cell training, metabolite regulation) to explain *how* the microbiome affects tumor immunity and treatment response. Useful for detailing the biological plausibility of microbiome-ICI interactions.",
      "role": "planning",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::592aefafd208990b34ed0f86ee32fd5bca51feaa3368169e179a15c1fe4dd9eb",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "1",
            "possible_uses"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::0520e8e0919b8dfe49e849560087258393e41ea69f2975f48a7f741130e0325e",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::096f074afde0f7a936937feb": {
      "atom_id": "atom::096f074afde0f7a936937feb",
      "source_handle": "P0438",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "planning_summary"
      ],
      "value": "This review provides detailed mechanistic explanations and specific bacterial examples for how the gut microbiome modulates the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) and chemotherapy in solid tumors, particularly GI cancers. It offers concrete biological pathways (immune cell training, metabolite regulation) and lists specific bacterial taxa associated with positive or negative ICI responses, serving as a direct source for mechanism and context in the facet analysis.",
      "role": "planner_interpretation",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::592aefafd208990b34ed0f86ee32fd5bca51feaa3368169e179a15c1fe4dd9eb",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "planning_summary"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::e7671abd17a126428c1aa3c13092a901c5c3691278e3182536ac581dbeef6ea8",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::fe0fe2da86f5f5dc42931ae1": {
      "atom_id": "atom::fe0fe2da86f5f5dc42931ae1",
      "source_handle": "P0438",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "scope_interpretation_cautions"
      ],
      "value": "The review focuses primarily on Gastrointestinal Cancers (GC, CRC, PDAC). While it mentions ICIs are used in melanoma and renal cell carcinoma, the specific microbiome data provided is largely contextualized within GI oncology or general preclinical models. Caution should be taken when extrapolating specific GI microbiome findings to non-GI solid tumors without verifying cross-tumor applicability.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::592aefafd208990b34ed0f86ee32fd5bca51feaa3368169e179a15c1fe4dd9eb",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions",
            "0"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::087236e9a2f8b26c822a3c4bbb5cf03fbd1914ebd4bd11d6fb4089c4c72c79dc",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::19c20d06cae659d317987aca": {
      "atom_id": "atom::19c20d06cae659d317987aca",
      "source_handle": "P0438",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "scope_interpretation_cautions"
      ],
      "value": "Mechanistic descriptions often rely on bacterial virulence factors (e.g., CagA, BFT) driving inflammation/carcinogenesis. These are specific to certain infections (H. pylori, ETBF) and may not represent the general mechanism of all microbiome-ICI interactions, which might be more dependent on commensal metabolic outputs.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::592aefafd208990b34ed0f86ee32fd5bca51feaa3368169e179a15c1fe4dd9eb",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions",
            "1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::087236e9a2f8b26c822a3c4bbb5cf03fbd1914ebd4bd11d6fb4089c4c72c79dc",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::9849449b61645e678a341cca": {
      "atom_id": "atom::9849449b61645e678a341cca",
      "source_handle": "P0438",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "scope_interpretation_cautions"
      ],
      "value": "The review acknowledges individual variability in microbiome composition as a major limitation. Findings are presented as associations or potential mechanisms rather than definitive causal proofs for all patient populations.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::592aefafd208990b34ed0f86ee32fd5bca51feaa3368169e179a15c1fe4dd9eb",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "scope_interpretation_cautions",
            "2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::087236e9a2f8b26c822a3c4bbb5cf03fbd1914ebd4bd11d6fb4089c4c72c79dc",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
