    },
    "atom::c38c95af14abb4799933f6ba": {
      "atom_id": "atom::c38c95af14abb4799933f6ba",
      "source_handle": "P0559",
      "field_path": [
        "study_summary_A",
        "problem_or_question"
      ],
      "value": "To systematically summarize and analyze the diverse roles of microbial-derived metabolites in shaping anti-tumor immunity and modulating the efficacy of major cancer therapies (immunotherapy and chemotherapy), thereby identifying potential therapeutic targets and biomarkers.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::9c38714850a8edbe294a4d75f35476ffa83fb98c43dddaf9cc0f3fd8a7f3289c",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "problem_or_question"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::cf9f8a8637dbf40193d90e1137927f819cb70d1442c6bc19f936556fb7e54f76",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::d62a6e78ffa6bd5524f54b6e": {
      "atom_id": "atom::d62a6e78ffa6bd5524f54b6e",
      "source_handle": "P0559",
      "field_path": [
        "study_summary_A",
        "research_scope"
      ],
      "value": "The review focuses on microbial-derived metabolites (specifically SCFAs, tryptophan metabolites, TMAO, urolithins, formate, DAT, and succinic acid) and their regulatory roles in anti-tumor immunity, cancer immunotherapy (particularly immune checkpoint inhibitors), and chemotherapy outcomes across various cancer types.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::9c38714850a8edbe294a4d75f35476ffa83fb98c43dddaf9cc0f3fd8a7f3289c",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "research_scope"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::a86cfa6ecac776abc187526b568f637b380ca0dc05aca24dce3db736cd749f3e",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::fb8823d1bac67f48579eecd8": {
      "atom_id": "atom::fb8823d1bac67f48579eecd8",
      "source_handle": "P0559",
      "field_path": [
        "study_summary_A",
        "work_summary"
      ],
      "value": "This is a comprehensive narrative review that synthesizes current literature on how gut microbiota-derived metabolites influence cancer biology and treatment responses. It categorizes key metabolites, detailing their origins, mechanisms of action within the tumor microenvironment (TME), and their specific impacts on immune cell function (e.g., CD8+ T cells, Tregs, macrophages). The review highlights both beneficial effects (enhancing immunotherapy efficacy, sensitizing tumors to chemotherapy) and detrimental effects (inducing resistance, promoting immunosuppression), providing a mechanistic framework for understanding microbiome-immune-tumor interactions.",
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::9c38714850a8edbe294a4d75f35476ffa83fb98c43dddaf9cc0f3fd8a7f3289c",
          "field_path": [
            "study_summary_A",
            "work_summary"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::d965981dc49564d744eb50ca43d72da79eb4b976976d4cbc37cf50fdddec003b",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::a91efcf335aaa13a883e57b3": {
      "atom_id": "atom::a91efcf335aaa13a883e57b3",
      "source_handle": "P0559",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "planning_summary"
      ],
      "value": "This review serves as a high-level mechanistic map for the user's综述, specifically addressing how gut microbiome metabolites modulate immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) efficacy in solid tumors. It provides concrete examples of metabolites that either sensitize tumors to ICIs (e.g., Butyrate/PD-1, TMAO/PD-1, DAT/CTLA-4) or confer resistance (e.g., Butyrate/CTLA-4, Succinic acid/PD-1). It offers detailed molecular mechanisms (receptors, signaling pathways, epigenetic changes) that can be used to answer the 'how' and 'what mechanisms' facets of the user's query.",
      "role": "planner_interpretation",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::9c38714850a8edbe294a4d75f35476ffa83fb98c43dddaf9cc0f3fd8a7f3289c",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "planning_summary"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::e214801a77e89acbeaea70e464b665a171153ad78199af64b7937285753b60bb",
      "multiple_values_in_field": false
    },
    "atom::06e260e6ced336c2a4cf02b7": {
      "atom_id": "atom::06e260e6ced336c2a4cf02b7",
      "source_handle": "P0559",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "Most evidence comes from mouse models (e.g., Kang et al., Wang et al., Joachim et al.). Human clinical data is often correlational (e.g., Zhu et al. on serum butyrate and PD-1 expression). Causality in humans is not fully established.",
        "contribution": "Identifies specific metabolites associated with improved or reduced ICI efficacy. For example, it links Butyrate to enhanced anti-PD-1 response (via H3K27ac) but reduced anti-CTLA-4 response (via Treg induction). It also links TMAO and DAT to enhanced ICI responses, while Succinic acid and Formate are linked to resistance.",
        "facet_id": "F1"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::9c38714850a8edbe294a4d75f35476ffa83fb98c43dddaf9cc0f3fd8a7f3289c",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::2c2a22ecbb468a6adf28b225dcc7cdccb831e591e2598c536d3ac3522e6c1aca",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::b126ce1c6a262e018d09e7ae": {
      "atom_id": "atom::b126ce1c6a262e018d09e7ae",
      "source_handle": "P0559",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions",
        "possible_uses"
      ],
      "value": "Can be used to list positive and negative predictive metabolites for ICI outcomes. Helps distinguish between different ICI types (PD-1 vs CTLA-4) where metabolite effects diverge.",
      "role": "planning",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::9c38714850a8edbe294a4d75f35476ffa83fb98c43dddaf9cc0f3fd8a7f3289c",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "0",
            "possible_uses"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::6d839bb6eccb90f2a506d7670adc44757dca6ea86de61126f47e03bd623e13f6",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::1b75395d66392d4c131fbb5a": {
      "atom_id": "atom::1b75395d66392d4c131fbb5a",
      "source_handle": "P0559",
      "field_path": [
        "review_planning_B",
        "facet_contributions"
      ],
      "value": {
        "boundaries": "Mechanisms are often described in isolated cell types or mouse models. The complexity of the TME means these single-pathway descriptions may not capture all interactions. Some mechanisms (e.g., Butyrate's dual role) depend on context (ICI type, dose, timing).",
        "contribution": "Details the molecular mechanisms: Butyrate acts via HDAC inhibition, GPCR activation (HCA2), TLR5 binding, and epigenetic regulation of Cd28/Pdcd1. TMAO activates PERK kinase and Type I IFN pathway. DAT activates IFN-I signaling. Succinic acid inhibits cGAS-interferon-beta pathway. ILA regulates epigenetic marks (H3K27ac) on IL12a enhancers.",
        "facet_id": "F2"
      },
      "role": "evidence",
      "origins": [
        {
          "record_id": "record::9c38714850a8edbe294a4d75f35476ffa83fb98c43dddaf9cc0f3fd8a7f3289c",
          "field_path": [
            "review_planning_B",
            "facet_contributions",
            "1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "variant_group": "field::2c2a22ecbb468a6adf28b225dcc7cdccb831e591e2598c536d3ac3522e6c1aca",
      "multiple_values_in_field": true
    },
    "atom::96e3f6945ee974ede6f393d6": {
      "atom_id": "atom::96e3f6945ee974ede6f393d6",
